## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 3f3305e469b9f5b4a21215fec35b8ef8ea0e6add

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I traced the history of the modified line in `Minecraft.java` using `git log -p --follow` and `git log -S"isOnline"`. The fix (be39b28) changes an `if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){ pause(); } ...` condition to also check `!isOnline()`. I searched the log for when that `if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){...}` block around the ESC key handler was first introduced.

**(2) Specific change:** Commit `3f3305e469b9f5b4a21215fec35b8ef8ea0e6add` ("Sun Jul 19 11:12:52 2015") changed the ESC-key handler from an unconditional `pause();` to:
```java
if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){
    pause();
} else {
    Mouse.setGrabbed(false);
}
```
This made pausing conditional on `packetHandler.isLoadingLevel` being false, without accounting for the fact that `isLoadingLevel` is also relevant/true-by-default outside of multiplayer network sessions.

**(3) Supporting evidence:** Looking at `PacketHandler.java`, `isLoadingLevel` is initialized to `true` in the constructor and is only ever set back to `false` inside network-packet-handling code (`handlePacket`, around lines 166 and 297). In offline/singleplayer sessions, these packet-handling code paths aren't driven by a live network connection in the same way, so `isLoadingLevel` could remain `true` indefinitely, causing the ESC key to merely ungrab the mouse instead of actually pausing the game. The fix in be39b28 adds `|| !isOnline()` so that offline games always pause on ESC regardless of the `isLoadingLevel` flag's state — directly patching the faulty assumption introduced in 3f3305e.

I'm reasonably confident in this identification since the diff context in be39b28 is the exact same `if` condition/line introduced in 3f3305e, and no intervening commit altered this specific condition (only `f0e850e` changed an unrelated nearby line changing `Mouse.setGrabbed(false)` to `pause()` for a different ESC branch). There is low uncertainty here.

